CAT = "px5Writing"; SEG = "WRI"
Q = [
("Writing that states a position and gives reasons to persuade a reader is:",
 "Opinion writing", ["Narrative writing", "Informative writing", "Descriptive writing"],
 "Opinion (in later grades, argument) writing takes a stance and supports it with reasons. Informative writing explains a topic without arguing a side."),
("A text that explains the steps for planting bean seeds is which type of writing?",
 "Informative/explanatory", ["Opinion/argument", "Narrative", "Poetic"],
 "A how-to text informs the reader by explaining a process. Narrative writing tells a story of real or imagined events."),
("A student writes a thank-you letter to the mayor full of slang and emojis. The main problem with the letter is:",
 "Its tone does not suit the reader", ["Its topic sentence is too weak", "It has too few supporting details", "It has no clear sequence"],
 "Effective writing fits its task, purpose and audience. A letter to a public official calls for a formal, respectful register."),
("During which stage of the writing process does a student mainly reorganize ideas and add details?",
 "Revising", ["Editing", "Prewriting", "Publishing"],
 "Revising improves content and organization. Editing comes later and fixes surface errors such as spelling and punctuation."),
("Correcting spelling, capitalization and punctuation belongs to which stage of the writing process?",
 "Editing", ["Revising", "Drafting", "Prewriting"],
 "Editing is the proofreading stage for conventions. Revising changes ideas and structure rather than mechanics."),
("Which statement about the writing process is accurate?",
 "Writers return to earlier stages", ["Each stage is done only once", "Editing comes before drafting", "Publishing comes before revising"],
 "Planning, drafting, revising and editing are recursive: a writer who revises may return to planning or drafting before publishing."),
("A kindergartner writes \"I lk m dg\" for \"I like my dog.\" This writing shows which developmental spelling stage?",
 "Semiphonetic", ["Precommunicative", "Transitional", "Conventional"],
 "Semiphonetic spellers use a few letters, mostly consonants, to stand for the main sounds in a word. Precommunicative writing uses letters with no link to sounds."),
("A second grader spells \"hiked\" as \"hikked\" and \"eagle\" as \"eagul\", putting a vowel in every syllable and using familiar patterns wrongly. This writer is in which stage?",
 "Transitional", ["Semiphonetic", "Precommunicative", "Phonetic"],
 "Transitional spellers know common spelling patterns and include vowels in every syllable but overapply or misplace them. Phonetic spellers write sounds without regard to patterns."),
("Which digital tool lets several students write and edit the same document at the same time?",
 "A shared online document", ["A slideshow saved on one laptop", "A printed worksheet", "An offline spell checker"],
 "Cloud-based documents allow real-time collaboration, comments and version history. A file saved on one device can be edited by only one person at a time."),
("Which digital tool is designed mainly for students to post ideas and reply to one another on a topic?",
 "A discussion board", ["A spreadsheet", "A word processor", "A photo editor"],
 "Discussion boards support written interaction among many people. Word processors and spreadsheets are tools for producing documents and data."),
("After choosing a research question, what should a student usually do next?",
 "Locate and evaluate sources", ["Write the final draft", "Present the findings", "Publish the finished report"],
 "Research moves from a question to gathering and judging sources, then taking notes, drafting and citing. Drafting before finding sources skips the evidence."),
("A student is researching the 1906 San Francisco earthquake. Which of these is a primary source?",
 "A survivor's diary entry", ["A textbook chapter", "An encyclopedia entry", "A recent magazine article"],
 "A primary source is created by someone who took part in or witnessed the events. Textbooks, encyclopedias and later articles are secondary sources that interpret them."),
("Which website is likely the most reliable source of facts about local weather?",
 "A national weather service site", ["An anonymous personal blog", "A product advertisement page", "A stranger's post on social media"],
 "Reliable sources have expert authors, a clear purpose of informing, and accountability. Anonymous posts and ads lack those signs of credibility."),
("A student rewrites an author's paragraph in her own words and cites the author. This is:",
 "Paraphrasing", ["Plagiarizing", "Direct quoting", "Copying"],
 "Paraphrasing restates ideas in new words and still credits the source. Quoting keeps the exact words inside quotation marks."),
("Changing a few words of a source and then turning it in without a citation is:",
 "Plagiarism", ["Paraphrasing", "Summarizing", "Fair quotation"],
 "Using another writer's ideas or wording without credit is plagiarism, even if some words are changed. A true paraphrase is in the student's own words and is cited."),
("In a narrative, transition phrases such as \"later that night\" and \"the next morning\" mainly help to:",
 "Show the sequence of events", ["State the writer's opinion", "Define key vocabulary", "Cite the writer's sources"],
 "Time transitions organize a narrative in order and move the reader smoothly from one event to the next."),
]
